apture nearly 14 billion cubic meters  of natural gas which even currently  flares vents or leaks from its oil and  gas operations  and because of this cooperation Egypt  has elevated its climate ambition and  submitting enhanced National  determination contributions  if we're going to win this fight  every major emitter Nation needs to  align with a 1.5 degrees we can no  longer plead ignorance  to the consequences of our actions or  continue to repeat our mistakes  everyone has to keep accelerating  efforts throughout this decisive decade  well let's take a look at some of the  day's other climate news now and just  stop oil have suspended their protests  on the M25 after causing days of  widespread disruption on the major  Motorway that was circling London now  their supporters have been climbing  overhead gantries in several locations  forcing the police to close sections of  the road for safety while officers  remove activists now the groups say  they're putting the campaign on hold to  give the government time to consider  their responsibilities  now the European Union has agreed to a  law to expand its forests marshes and  other sinks that absorb carbon dioxide  it's a measure that could allow the  block to increase its Target for cutting  net emissions of greenhouse gases now  currently EU countries have to ensure  they compensate emissions from land use  and Forestry with at least an equivalent  amount of carbon removal and under new  law removals of CO2 will need to exceed  emissions from 2026.  Scotland and Northern Ireland have  experienced their warmest November night  on record that's according to the Met  Office provisional figures of course now  average November minimum temperatures  are around three to four degrees Celsius  at this time of year but current  temperatures are well over 10 degrees  warmer than they should be  now move or face falling into the sea  that's a dilemma which could soon face  residents of some of the UK's Coastal  communities as rising sea levels cause  Coastal erosion to accelerate across the  country now experts warn that by 2050  more than 200 000 properties around the  UK's Coastline will be at risk  potentially creating thousands of  climate refugees our science  correspondent Thomas Moore reports  the Sandy Cliffs of Norfolk are a feeble  defense against climate change sea  levels are rising speeding up natural  erosion and the village of hayesboro has  lost 40 houses in just three decades  I've lived here nearly 20 years now when  Nicola's family bought their house they  were told it would be safe for 150 years  but the sea has advanced so fast she  fears it could be gone in 10 if she's  lucky when the Seas hitting the cliff  you can the house trembles you know you  can that's how close I am now that it's  the strength of the sea you wouldn't  believe  how it can just destroy and it's just  mud and Clay here we just it's just  constantly chomping away every time we  have a high tide sea levels around the  UK have already risen by 16 centimeters  in the last 20 years but the pace is  picking up by 2050 they're predicted to  rise by 35 centimeters and by the end of  the century they're likely to have risen  by well over a meter but even that could  be an underestimate the sea will rise  much further much faster if the vast ice  sheets that blanket the polar regions  start to crumble and melt even more  rapidly scientists believe we're  approaching that Tipping Point and if it  starts it can't be stopped  hayesboro is going over the edge now but  by 2050 200 000 properties will be at  risk and the government says it's too  expensive to defend the whole Coastline  experts warn home owners aren't being  told whether they're vulnerable what  we're seeing is that the price of  coastal property Cliff top property  property at risk of flooding is not  reflecting the um the risk to which  people are exposed there needs to be  really a much more in the way of an  honest conversation around